Replace the Application-wide overrides with a Window subclass and return one instance for each window:

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
        => new AppWindow(new AppShell());
}

public sealed class AppWindow : Window
{
    public AppWindow(Page page) : base(page)
    {
        Created += HandleCreated;
    }

    private static void HandleCreated(object? sender, EventArgs e)
        => AppServices.EnsureStarted(); // old OnStart intent

    protected override void OnStopped()
    {
        base.OnStopped();
        DraftState.Save(); // old OnSleep intent
    }

    protected override void OnResumed()
    {
        base.OnResumed();
        DraftState.Restore(); // old OnResume intent
    }
}
```

Window.Created preserves one-time startup intent for each new window, OnStopped is the real background transition, and OnResumed is a return after Stopped. OnActivated is different because it can recur whenever a window regains focus. Keeping these callbacks on each Window prevents one document window from representing every window in an iPad or desktop process.
